    "answer": {
      "short": "$y=f\\left(x-\\dfrac{d}{\\sqrt{1+a^2}}\\right)+\\dfrac{ad}{\\sqrt{1+a^2}}$.\n\nResolve the distance into horizontal and vertical components, then replace $x$ and $y$.",
      "worked": "Let the horizontal displacement be $h$ and the vertical displacement be $k$. The direction gives $k=ah$, and motion towards increasing $x$ selects the nonnegative horizontal component.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h^2+(ah)^2&=d^2\\\\h^2(1+a^2)&=d^2\\\\h&=\\frac{d}{\\sqrt{1+a^2}}\\\\k&=ah\\\\k&=\\frac{ad}{\\sqrt{1+a^2}}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nReplace $x$ with $x-h$ and $y$ with $y-k$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y-\\frac{ad}{\\sqrt{1+a^2}}&=f\\left(x-\\frac{d}{\\sqrt{1+a^2}}\\right)\\\\y&=f\\left(x-\\frac{d}{\\sqrt{1+a^2}}\\right)+\\frac{ad}{\\sqrt{1+a^2}}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
